<p>British police arrested Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor (formerly Prince Andrew), King Charles III’s brother, on Thursday, February 19. They held him for about 10–12 hours, then released him “under investigation,” meaning no charge has been filed while inquiries continue. Police said the arrest relates to suspected misconduct in public office, and reports have linked the inquiry to newly released U.S. Justice Department material connected to Jeffrey Epstein. The case has drawn intense attention because it appears to be the first arrest of a senior British royal in modern times. It also raises wider questions about public trust in the monarchy and whether prominent figures face the same legal process as anyone else. King Charles urged cooperation with authorities and said the matter should run its course through the courts, while Andrew has denied wrongdoing.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/rsBSajJxXkOnF125jfmcgDIy9WaBQx0ZLCFk3owVdhcI90R3-pWfhzGeh9COM6Xo4jTxFDHiKHT8kWD1ldOrudWbPCUQjirVXLrukDHl8MerVT-Y3Oxw4wDCfvXMFnrCJXIuoji5YRDQpy_GvEKgoWVPqCicAIKvx03rrNNExyPDw_ddze4eM_kvhwquuWuIQTg-IIsg5rVgsg-6U_M' alt='Police officers at an entrance to Wood Farm on the royal family’s Sandringham Estate on Thursday, where Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor was living.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Search activity: Police arrested Andrew at Wood Farm on the Sandringham estate and searched the property, along with addresses in Berkshire, according to reporting on the operation.</li><li>Timing and precedent: Several reports noted the arrest fell on Andrew’s 66th birthday, with some outlets calling it the first senior-royal arrest in roughly four centuries.</li><li>First images after release: Reporting highlighted a Reuters photo of Andrew leaving Aylsham police station after 7 p.m. local time, slumped in the back of a Range Rover and appearing shaken.</li><li>Broadcast coverage: The BBC posted video recapping the day, including footage of the former prince spending hours at a Norfolk police station before his release.</li><li>Investigative focus: Coverage has centered on whether Andrew, in a past official capacity (including as a trade envoy), may have shared or passed along confidential material to Epstein—an issue raised in reporting tied to newly disclosed U.S. case documents.</li></ul><blockquote>Let me state clearly: the law must take its course. - King Charles III</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Thames Valley Police: Police said they arrested “a man in his sixties from Norfolk” on suspicion of misconduct in public office, then released him “under investigation.” (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/uk-police-says-ex-prince-andrew-released-under-investigation/live-76032665'>DW</a>)</li><li>King Charles III: The King said the family would support and cooperate with authorities, and stressed that the legal process should proceed. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-king-charles-statement-prince-andrew-arrest-2026/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor: Andrew has denied wrongdoing as the investigation continues. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/19/why-was-former-prince-andrew-arrested-by-uk-police'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Virginia Giuffre’s family: Family members said they are grateful police acted, while also pressing for broader accountability tied to the Epstein case. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/02/19/nx-s1-5719251/family-of-virginia-giuffre-reacts-to-arrest-of-former-prince-andrew'>NPR</a>)</li><li>Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese: Albanese called Andrew’s arrest an “extraordinary fall from grace,” but said it would not prompt another Australian referendum on becoming a republic. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/feb/20/pm-anthony-albanese-australia-republic-comments-former-prince-andrew-arrest-ntwnfb'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/videos/cvg192yp9geo'>Watch: The day Andrew Mount